\begin{proof}
Let $T$ be a binary phylogenetic tree on a taxon set $X$ and let $e_1$ be an interior edge adjacent to a cherry. The tree is connected, which implies we can label the remaining interior edges $\{e_2, \cdots, e_{n-3} \}$ such that $e_j$ is adjacent to some $e_i$ with $i <j$.

Let $e_j$ be adjacent to $e_i$ with $i<j$. We know that $e_i$ separates $T$ into two connected components $T_i^a$ and $T_i^b$. Moreover, $e_j$ separates $T$ into two connected components $T_j^a$ and $T_j^b$. Because $e_i$ and $e_j$ are adjacent, $supp(T_i^b) \cap supp(T_j^a) \neq \emptyset$. Let $q_i=ab|cd$ and $q_j=ac|de$ such that $a \in supp(T_i^a)$, $c \in supp(T_j^b)$, and $d \in supp(T_i^b) \cap supp(T_j^a)$. Thus, $q_i$ and $q_j$ are linked for all $e_i$ and $e_j$ in $T$. Thus, we have a linked system that makes up the first row of our matrix. 

Using quartet tree substitution, construct vines $v(q_i)$ and $v(q_j)$. By Theorem \ref{linkedquartets}  the vines  $v(q_j)$ and $v(q_i)$ are linked. Thus, we have $n-3$ disjoint columns of quartet trees in our matrix. Additionally, for each pair of linked quartet trees $\{q_i,q_j\}$ in row one, there exists a pair $\{q_i(x),q_j(x)\}$ in the remaining rows of the matrix that are linked. Thus, we have $n-3$ rows of linked systems.  

Therefore, $Q_T$ contains a meshed system. 
\end{proof}

